course: biology
lesson: 331
title: Foods that are dangerous for pets: chocolate, grapes, onions and lilies
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A dog's body and a cat's body are built differently from yours, so some foods you eat happily work like poison inside them.
explain: Picture Easter morning: chocolate eggs on the coffee table, the family out in the garden, and the dog's nose already twitching. A bar that gives you a treat can send a dog to the vet, because its body clears one chemical in chocolate very slowly and it builds up.
explain: The same goes for raisins in a hot cross bun, onion in the gravy, a dropped piece of sugar-free gum, and even the yellow dust from lilies in a vase, which a cat licks off its fur without meaning to.
explain: The safe rule is simple: keep human snacks out of reach, read labels before sharing, and if a pet eats something dangerous, call the vet at once, even before the pet looks ill.
fact: Chocolate contains a chemical that dogs break down very slowly, so it builds up in the body and makes them ill; dark chocolate and cocoa powder are the most dangerous.
fact: Vets treat many dogs for eating chocolate at Christmas and Easter, when there is lots of chocolate lying around the house.
fact: Grapes, raisins and sultanas can badly damage a dog's kidneys; some dogs get very ill from a few and others less, so no amount is known to be safe.
fact: Scientists recently reported that a natural acid in grapes, called tartaric acid, may be what harms dogs.
fact: Onions, garlic, leeks and chives damage red blood cells in cats and dogs, and cooked, dried or powdered onion is still dangerous; onion powder hides in gravy, stock and many ready meals.
fact: Lilies are very dangerous to cats: licking a little pollen off the fur or drinking the water from the vase can harm a cat's kidneys, so a cat that touches lilies needs a vet at once.
fact: Xylitol, a sweetener in some sugar-free gum and sweets, makes the sugar in a dog's blood, the fuel its body runs on, drop fast, and it can be deadly to dogs, though it is fine for people.
fact: Cooked bones splinter into sharp pieces that can hurt a dog's throat and gut; chews made for dogs are safer, whatever the cartoons show.
fact: Avocado contains a chemical that can be very dangerous for pet birds, and salt and chocolate are bad for birds too; budgies need seeds, greens and safe fruit.
fact: Macadamia nuts can make dogs weak, wobbly and sick for a day or two, and nobody knows exactly why.
fact: A Christmas kitchen holds many pet dangers at once: chocolate, raisins in pudding and mince pies, onions in stuffing, cooked bones and alcohol.
fact: If a pet eats something dangerous, call a vet straight away and take the packet along; never try to make a pet sick yourself, only a vet should decide.
exercise: Why is chocolate dangerous for dogs? | Dogs break down one of its chemicals very slowly, so it builds up | Dogs cannot taste sweetness, so they eat far too much of it and get very fat | Chocolate sticks to a dog's teeth and blocks its throat | Chocolate makes a dog's fur fall out within a day
exercise: Which kind of chocolate is the most dangerous for a dog? | Dark chocolate and cocoa powder | White chocolate | Milk chocolate with nuts | Chocolate that has gone soft in the sun
exercise: When do vets treat the most dogs for eating chocolate? | At Christmas and Easter | In the middle of summer | On the first day of school | On rainy autumn weekends
exercise: What can grapes, raisins and sultanas harm in a dog? | Its kidneys | Its ears | Its claws | Its tail
exercise: How many raisins are known to be safe for a dog? | None, because some dogs get very ill from a few | Any number at all, as long as they are cooked in a bun | Up to ten for a big dog | Only the green ones are dangerous
exercise: What did scientists recently report might make grapes harmful to dogs? | A natural acid in grapes | The pips inside the grapes | Sprays farmers put on the vines | The grape skins being too tough to chew
exercise: Which of these is dangerous for cats and dogs because it harms red blood cells? | Onion | Cucumber | Plain rice | Carrot
exercise: Is onion still dangerous after it has been cooked or dried into powder? | Yes, cooked, dried and powdered onion are all still dangerous | No, cooking or drying takes the danger out and makes it completely safe | No, only raw onion skin is harmful | Only fried onion is still dangerous
exercise: Why can gravy and ready meals be a hidden danger for pets? | They often contain onion powder | They are always too hot to eat | They contain chocolate | They are made from grapes
exercise: How can lilies harm a cat that never bites the flowers? | It licks the pollen off its fur or drinks the vase water | The strong smell alone makes its whiskers and fur fall out | The petals scratch its eyes | The cat gets stuck in the stems
exercise: What should happen if a cat touches lilies? | It needs a vet at once | It needs a bath and a nap | Nothing, unless it eats a whole flower | It should be given milk to drink
exercise: What does the xylitol in some sugar-free gum do to a dog? | Makes its blood sugar drop fast | Makes it sleep for a whole week | Makes its teeth turn blue | Makes its fur sticky
exercise: Why should a dog not be given a cooked chicken bone? | Cooked bones splinter into sharp pieces | Cooked bones make the dog's teeth grow too long | Cooked bones are too soft to chew | Cooked bones have no taste at all
exercise: Which food can be very dangerous for a pet budgie? | Avocado | Millet seed | Lettuce | A slice of apple without pips
exercise: What do macadamia nuts do to many dogs? | Make them weak, wobbly and sick for a day or two | Make them run much faster and bark all night long | Make their fur shine | Make them bark all night
exercise: Which Christmas foods are dangerous for a dog? | Chocolate, mince pies, stuffing with onion and cooked bones | Plain boiled carrots, green beans and the cranberries on the side | Only the Christmas crackers | Only the turkey skin
exercise: The dog has eaten raisins but seems fine. What should you do? | Call the vet straight away and keep the packet | Wait until the dog looks ill | Make the dog sick yourself | Give it lots of chocolate to calm it
test: «dog nicked half a box of raisins, he's totally fine, chill» What is right? | Call the vet now; raisins can hurt the kidneys before he looks ill | Give him lots of water and forget about it, raisins just pass through | He looks fine, so he is safe | Raisins are only dangerous to cats
test: «just a lil bit of gravy on the cat's biscuits, it's only meat juice innit» What is the problem? | Gravy often hides onion powder, which harms cats' blood | There is no problem at all, gravy is just meat juice and water | Gravy makes a cat's whiskers fall out | Cats cannot drink anything brown
test: In a cartoon a dog trots off happily with a huge cooked bone and buries it. What is wrong with this as advice? | Cooked bones splinter and can hurt a dog's throat and gut | Nothing, a big cooked bone is the best treat a dog can have | Dogs never bury anything | Dogs cannot carry bones in their mouths
test: Mum puts lilies on the kitchen table where the cat walks. What would happen if the cat brushed past and then washed itself? | It could swallow pollen and need a vet at once | Nothing, because cats only eat meat | The pollen would dye its fur yellow for good | The cat would sneeze once and be fine
test: Why can a food be safe for you and dangerous for a dog? | Animals' bodies break down chemicals in different ways | Food changes its taste and turns bad inside a dog's mouth | Dogs have more teeth than people | Dogs eat faster than people
test: «shut up about the gum, it's SUGAR-FREE, it's healthy» What is right about sugar-free gum and dogs? | Its xylitol can make a dog's blood sugar crash, so it is dangerous | Dogs cannot swallow gum, so even a whole packet is safe for them | Sugar-free means it is safe for every animal | Only gum with sugar can hurt dogs
test: Why is the chocolate danger biggest at Easter? | Eggs and bars lie about the house where dogs can reach them | Easter chocolate is made with onion and raisins for the holiday | Chocolate is stronger in spring | Dogs are hungrier at Easter
test: «gave the budgie a bit of my guacamole lol he loved it» Why is that worrying? | Avocado can be very dangerous for pet birds | Budgies cannot eat anything green | Guacamole is too cold for birds | Budgies only drink water
test: Count them: two dogs and a cat crowd round the Christmas table hoping for scraps. How many tails and how many legs are under the table? | 3 tails and 12 legs | 3 tails and 6 legs | 2 tails and 12 legs | 3 tails and 9 legs
test: «where do i put the macadamias, there's a dog» Where is best? | High in a closed cupboard the dog cannot reach | On the low coffee table | In a bowl on the floor | In the dog's bed so he can guard them
test: The dog ate something from the bin and you do not know what. What should you take to the vet? | The packet or wrapper, so the vet knows what and how much | A bag of the dog's favourite chocolate to keep it calm at the vet's | Nothing, the vet can guess | A bowl of salt water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard posed by chocolate to domestic dogs? | A constituent of chocolate is eliminated very slowly by the canine body and accumulates to harmful levels | Chocolate is hazardous to dogs solely on account of its sugar content, which causes immediate damage to the teeth | Chocolate presents a hazard exclusively when consumed in a frozen state | Chocolate is harmless to dogs provided it is of the dark variety
exam: Identify the course of action prescribed in the event that a dog is known to have ingested raisins. | Immediate consultation with a veterinary surgeon, irrespective of the animal's apparent condition | Postponement of any action until symptoms of illness become manifest | The administration of an emetic by the owner without professional advice | The provision of additional dried fruit to dilute the effect
exam: Ascertain the principal hazard to a domestic cat arising from the presence of lilies within the dwelling. | The ingestion of pollen or vase water, which may cause grave injury to the kidneys | The obstruction of the cat's airways by the fragrance of the flowers, which may prove fatal within hours | The permanent discolouration of the cat's coat | The entanglement of the cat's limbs in the stems
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning onions in the diet of cats and dogs. | Onions remain harmful to the blood of such animals whether raw, cooked or dried | Onions are harmful only when consumed raw | Onions are beneficial to the blood of such animals | Onions are harmful exclusively to animals of advanced age
